Consider a thin anisotropic layer whose optic axis rotates
from $0$ to $2\pi/3$ over \SI{2}{\micro\metre}.
If the extraordinary–ordinary index contrast is $\eta=0.02$,
the path‐integrated retardance at \SI{650}{\nano\metre} is
\[
\Delta\phi=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}
\int_{-10\,\mu\mathrm{m}}^{+10\,\mu\mathrm{m}}\!\!\!
\eta\sin^{2}\!\theta(x)\,dx
