\begin{abstract}
A scalar misalignment profile $\theta_4(x)$ of the form
$\theta_4(x) = \frac{2\pi}{3}\cdot \frac{1 + \tanh(\mu x)}{2}$
produces a smooth refractive index modulation
$\Delta n(x) = \eta \sin^2\theta_4(x)$. For $\mu = \SI{5}{\micro\metre^{-1}}$,
$\eta = 0.02$, and light of wavelength \SI{650}{\nano\metre}, the resulting
single-pass phase delay is $\Delta \phi \approx 1.48$ radians.
This note reproduces that result numerically and presents a standard
optical interpretation that is experimentally testable using simple
birefringent film stacks or photo-aligned liquid crystal layers.
\end{abstract}

\section{Model Setup}
We define a scalar field kink:
\begin{equation}
\theta_4(x) = \frac{2\pi}{3}\cdot \frac{1 + \tanh(\mu x)}{2},
\end{equation}
which modulates the refractive index by:
\begin{equation}
\Delta n(x) = \eta \sin^2 \theta_4(x).
\end{equation}
The accumulated optical phase shift is then:
\begin{equation}
\Delta \phi = \frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \int_{x_{\min}}^{x_{\max}} \Delta n(x)\, dx.
\end{equation}

\section{Simulation}
\textbf{Parameters}:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
$\mu$ (wall steepness) & \SI{5}{\micro\metre^{-1}} \\
$\eta$ (index amplitude) & 0.02 \\
$\lambda$ (wavelength) & \SI{650}{\nano\metre} \\
$x$ domain & $[-10, +10]\,\si{\micro\metre}$ \\
Grid size & 1000 points \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Code (Python 3)}:
\begin{minted}[fontsize=\small, breaklines]{python}
import numpy as np
mu     = 5
eta    = 0.02
lam    = 650e-3
x      = np.linspace(-10, 10, 1000)
theta4 = (2*np.pi/3)*(1 + np.tanh(mu*x))/2
deltan = eta * np.sin(theta4)**2
dphi   = 2*np.pi/lam * np.trapz(deltan, x)
print(dphi)  # ≈ 1.475 rad
\end{minted}

\section{Result}
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{refractive_index_modulation.png}
\caption{Top: Angular field $\theta_4(x)$.
Bottom: Resulting index perturbation $\Delta n(x)$. Integrated phase shift is approximately 1.48 radians.}
\end{figure}

\section{Interpretation (Optics Context)}
Consider a film stack with an optic axis smoothly rotated from $0$ to $2\pi/3$ across ~\SI{20}{\micro\metre}. For $\Delta n = 0.02$, the accumulated retardance is:
\[
\Delta \phi \approx \frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \int \eta \sin^2(\theta(x)) dx \approx 1.48 \text{ rad}.
\]
This lies within the measurable range of interferometers using consumer imaging sensors.

\section{Experimental Note}
If a multilayer stack or LC cell is constructed to match this angular profile, a phase shift of approximately $0.24 \lambda$ should be observed. A deviation from this by more than ~30% would call the underlying assumptions into question.

