\section{Experimental Implications}
This result suggests that a film with a smoothly photo-aligned optic axis kink can produce a clean, localized retardance signal without periodic structure or stacking. For $\eta = 0.02$ and optical wavelength $\lambda = 650$\,nm, the predicted retardance is well within detection range of off-the-shelf interferometers, including DSLR-based Mach–Zehnder configurations.

Potential implementations:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Photo-aligned nematic LC layers with spatially controlled anchoring
  \item Stacked polymer retarders with continuous rotation
  \item Gradient-orientation metasurfaces
\end{itemize}

This configuration may serve as a testbed for gradient-birefringence calibration or retardance modulation in compact beam-steering elements.

